      "flag": "repeated_invocations",
      "in_span_max": 0,
      "measure": 0,
      "measure_of": "the largest number of occurrences of ONE tool+arguments signature, consecutively or within one local span",
      "note": "Repeated invocations are counted MECHANICALLY: the identical tool and identical arguments, by digest. A repeat is not a finding that the work was wasted -- polling until a condition holds, retrying a flaky step, and re-reading a file that changed all repeat legitimately, and this instrument cannot tell those from a stuck loop because that needs the state the transcript does not record. What it gives you is where to look. Matching is EXACT, which under-reports on purpose: a retry whose arguments differ by a timestamp or a page token does not match, and no normalisation is shipped because none of the obvious ones is grounded -- the same reason no threshold is shipped. So treat a count here as a floor. In a WINDOW there is one further floor: a run that began before the window's first kept turn is seen only from the window's start, so its length is truncated, and a repeat whose other occurrences are all older is not seen at all. Span indices are LOCAL to the window (see read_window) -- an in-span multiplicity from a window and one from a full read are not the same measurement and must not be compared.",
